4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{1}=-5.\n$$"] [17592186272109 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=0\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{10}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathbf{x}}{‖\\mathbf{x}‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"] [17592186272239 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{6} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $\\det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$ is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is invertible”; $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ only."] [17592186272349 "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has $8$ pivot columns” only."] [17592186317166 "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}+{p}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}+{p}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}+{p}_{4}\\cdot {q}_{4}+{p}_{5}\\cdot {q}_{5} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-10-4-4-9-10 \\\\  & =-37\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317169 "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=12,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & =12 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}\\cdot 2{x}^{-2}+4\\cdot (-8x)+12\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 2 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48+12 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ Since we need $x<6$, our final answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186317226 "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}+\\mathbf{s} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317387 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k+9 \\\\ 2k+18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k+9 &=0 \\\\ 2k+18 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-9.$"] [17592186317441 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{\\mathbf{b}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ A{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ A{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186317454 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$"] [17592186318978 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}^{′} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, only the $1$st column is a pivot column, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st column from the original matrix is ${\\mathbf{g}}_{1}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{g}}_{1}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186318982 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{f}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{f}}_{1},{\\mathbf{f}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186319342 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2k-8 \\\\ k+4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k-8 &=0 \\\\ k+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-4.$"] [17592186323706 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }(-\\mathbf{w})\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A4}\\hspace{0.222em}]} \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326761 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\},$\n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2},{\\mathbf{w}}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatri