 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4})-{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{4}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=5$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"]}
